CHAPTER_META = {'chapter_num': 1, 'title': 'We the Travellers - I', 'class': 'Class 5', 'subject': 'Mathematics (Joyful Mathematics, NEP 2020)', 'description': "Comprehensive 20-slide interactive animated laboratory exploring human travel evolution from walking to spacecraft, large numbers up to Ten Thousand (10,000), Indian place value chart (TTh, Th, H, T, O), comma placement, comparing and ordering numbers, digit swap puzzles, rounding off to nearest 10, 100, and 1,000, travel speed (Distance = Speed × Time), field trip vehicle capacity math, large numbers in everyday life, and pastime mathematics puzzles (River Crossing, Pebble Game, Digit Difference, and King's Horses)."}

SLIDES = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'We the Travellers', 'subtitle': 'Human Fascination with Travel from Ancient Times to Spacecraft', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Since the dawn of human history, people have loved to travel, explore new lands, meet distant communities, and discover the unknown. Thousands of years ago, the only way to travel was by walking on foot. Gradually, humans tamed animals like bullocks, horses, camels, and yaks to pull carts and carry heavy loads across deserts, grasslands, and mountain trails.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Ancient Travel (0 to 15 km/h)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Walking at 3–5 km/h, riding bullock carts at 4 km/h, or sailing timber boats propelled by wind and oars across rivers and coastal bays.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Modern Era (80 to 28,000 km/h)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">High-speed electric trains travelling at 160 km/h, commercial aeroplanes at 850 km/h, and spacecraft orbiting at 28,000 km/h.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">As travel speeds and distances increased, humans needed larger numbers to measure travel distances, count passengers, calculate vehicle fuel, and map trade routes across vast continents.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Welcome young mathematicians! Since the earliest days of human civilization, people have had an adventurous desire to travel, explore new lands, and trade with distant communities. In ancient times, the only way to travel was by walking on foot at a gentle pace of three to five kilometres per hour. Later, humans learned to domesticate animals like bullocks, horses, and camels, pulling wooden carts at four to five kilometres per hour, and building timber boats to cross rivers. But modern technology changed everything! Today, high-speed electric trains like the Vande Bharat glide across our country at one hundred sixty kilometres per hour. Commercial aeroplanes fly through the clouds at eight hundred fifty kilometres per hour, and spacecraft soar into orbit at a breathtaking speed of twenty-eight thousand kilometres per hour! When speeds and travel distances expanded from just ten kilometres to thousands and millions of kilometres, humans needed much larger numbers to count travellers, measure distances, calculate vehicle fuel, and chart transport networks. This is why we study numbers beyond thousands, entering the wondrous realm of ten thousand. Look at the timeline simulator on your screen, test the speeds of different eras, and observe how our world grew closer through the power of mathematics!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If an ancient traveller walked at an average speed of 4 km every hour, how far could they travel in 6 hours of steady walking?', 'options': ['10 km', '24 km', '40 km', '64 km'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Distance = Speed × Time. At 4 km per hour, walking for 6 hours covers 4 × 6 = 24 km.', 'study_insight': "In ancient times, a 24 km journey was considered a full day's travel between neighbouring villages."}, {'question': 'Which modern mode of transport travels at a speed of approximately 28,000 km per hour?', 'options': ['A bullock cart', 'A city bicycle', 'A spacecraft orbiting the Earth', 'A passenger bus'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'A spacecraft such as the International Space Station orbits Earth at approximately 28,000 km per hour, circling the entire globe every 90 minutes.', 'study_insight': 'At 28,000 km/h, a spacecraft travels about 7.7 kilometres in a single second!'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Ten Thousand', 'subtitle': 'Discovering 5-Digit Numbers: Moving from 9,000 to 10,000', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In Grade 4, you mastered 4-digit numbers up to 9,999. Now let us discover what happens when we add thousands successively. Imagine counting passenger seats on long-distance trains:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3 font-mono text-xs text-center space-y-1">\n                <span class="text-slate-400">1,000 → 2,000 → 3,000 → 4,000 → 5,000 → 6,000 → 7,000 → 8,000 → 9,000</span><br>\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">9,000 + 1,000 = 10,000 (Ten Thousand)</span>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Just as 10 Hundreds bundle together to make <strong>1 Thousand</strong> (10 × 100 = 1,000), bundling 10 Thousands together gives us <strong>1 Ten Thousand</strong> (10 × 1,000 = 10,000).</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-3 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>Key Milestone:</strong> 10,000 is the <em>smallest 5-digit number</em>. It is written as 1 followed by four zeroes (1, 0, 0, 0, 0). The largest 4-digit number is 9,999, and adding 1 to 9,999 gives exactly 10,000!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Let us understand how large numbers are born from smaller ones by looking at the base-ten building blocks. In primary school, you learned that ten single unit cubes bundle together to make one ten rod. Ten of these rods make one flat hundred square, and ten hundred squares stack together to form one solid thousand cube. Now, what happens when we continue this journey in thousands? Suppose you have nine thousand cubes, which is nine thousand. If you add one more thousand cube, you have ten thousand cubes. In our decimal number system, we never write ten digits in a single place value column. Instead, all ten thousand cubes bundle together to form a brand new, massive unit called Ten Thousand! We write ten thousand as one followed by four zeros: one, zero, zero, zero, zero. It is the smallest five-digit number in the world. Just one number before it is nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine, which is the largest four-digit number. When we add one to nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine, we arrive right at ten thousand! Use the interactive block counter to bundle thousands one by one and witness the birth of ten thousand!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What number do you get when you add 1 to the largest 4-digit number, 9,999?', 'options': ['9,990', '10,000 (Ten Thousand)', '100,000 (One Lakh)', '99,999'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Adding 1 to 9,999 gives 10,000, which is the smallest 5-digit number.', 'study_insight': '9,999 + 1 = 10,000. In the place value system, adding 1 causes a cascade of regrouping across all 4 columns into the Ten Thousand column.'}, {'question': 'How many thousands are bundled together to form Ten Thousand (10,000)?', 'options': ['5 Thousands', '10 Thousands', '100 Thousands', '1,000 Thousands'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '10 Thousands = 1 Ten Thousand (10 × 1,000 = 10,000). The base-10 system always bundles ten of any unit to make one of the next higher unit.', 'study_insight': '10 Ones = 1 Ten; 10 Tens = 1 Hundred; 10 Hundreds = 1 Thousand; 10 Thousands = 1 Ten Thousand.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Place Value', 'subtitle': 'The Indian Place Value Chart: TTh, Th, H, T, and O', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To read and write large numbers systematically, we use the <strong>Indian Place Value System</strong>. With 5-digit numbers, we add a brand-new column to the left of Thousands: <strong>Ten Thousands (TTh)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="overflow-x-auto my-3">\n                <table class="w-full text-center text-xs border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">\n                    <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-amber-400 font-bold">\n                        <tr>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">TTh (Ten Thousands)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Th (Thousands)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">H (Hundreds)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">T (Tens)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">O (Ones)</th>\n                        </tr>\n                    </thead>\n                    <tbody class="bg-slate-950/70 text-slate-200">\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-sky-400">3</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-sky-400">5</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-emerald-400">4</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-amber-400">2</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-rose-400">7</td>\n                        </tr>\n                    </tbody>\n                </table>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">In the number <strong>35,427</strong>:</p>\n            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 list-disc list-inside space-y-1 my-2">\n                <li>Digit <strong>3</strong> is in Ten Thousands place → Value = 3 × 10,000 = 30,000</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>5</strong> is in Thousands place → Value = 5 × 1,000 = 5,000</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>4</strong> is in Hundreds place → Value = 4 × 100 = 400</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>2</strong> is in Tens place → Value = 2 × 10 = 20</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>7</strong> is in Ones place → Value = 7 × 1 = 7</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Expanded Form: <strong>30,000 + 5,000 + 400 + 20 + 7 = 35,427</strong>.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us examine the Indian Place Value Chart for five-digit numbers. Every five-digit number has five distinct columns, read from right to left: Ones, Tens, Hundreds, Thousands, and our new column, Ten Thousands, abbreviated as T, T, h. Each step to the left increases the value of a digit by ten times! For instance, a digit in the Ones column represents single units. In the Tens column, it is worth ten times as much. In the Hundreds column, it is worth a hundred times. In the Thousands column, a thousand times. And in the Ten Thousands column, that same digit is worth ten thousand times its face value! Consider the number thirty-four thousand two hundred eighty-five. The digit three sits in the Ten Thousands place, giving it a value of thirty thousand. The digit four is in the Thousands place, representing four thousand. The digit two is in the Hundreds place, worth two hundred. The digit eight in Tens is eighty, and five in Ones is five. In expanded form, we write it as thirty thousand, plus four thousand, plus two hundred, plus eighty, plus five. Click on each spike of the interactive abacus on your screen to add beads and watch the expanded form build up dynamically!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the place value of the digit 7 in the number 47,820?', 'options': ['70', '700', '7,000 (Seven Thousand)', '70,000'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'In 47,820, the digit 7 is in the Thousands place, so its place value is 7 × 1,000 = 7,000.', 'study_insight': 'Position matters! The digit 4 in 47,820 is in the Ten Thousands place (value 40,000), while 7 is in the Thousands place (value 7,000).'}, {'question': 'What is the standard numeral for the expanded form: 60,000 + 3,000 + 500 + 80 + 2?', 'options': ['6,358', '63,582', '635,082', '60,358'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '6 Ten Thousands (60,000) + 3 Thousands (3,000) + 5 Hundreds (500) + 8 Tens (80) + 2 Ones (2) = 63,582.', 'study_insight': 'Combining place values reconstructs the standard numeral form of a number.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Writing Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Comma Placement and Reading Number Names in the Indian System', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To read and write large numbers easily without getting confused by a long line of digits, we use <strong>commas</strong> to separate groups of place values called <strong>periods</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Thousands Period (TTh, Th)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">The first two digits on the left represent thousands. Read together: e.g., <strong>48</strong> thousands in 48,215.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Ones Period (H, T, O)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">The last three digits represent hundreds, tens, and ones: e.g., <strong>215</strong> (two hundred fifteen).</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 my-3 text-xs text-amber-200">\n                <strong>Indian Comma Rule:</strong> In the Indian system, the <em>first comma</em> is placed after 3 digits from the right (separating the Ones period: H, T, O). Subsequent commas are placed after every 2 digits! For a 5-digit number, we have exactly one comma: <strong>XX,XXX</strong>.\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Examples:</p>\n            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 list-disc list-inside space-y-1">\n                <li><strong>24,560</strong> = Twenty-four thousand, five hundred sixty</li>\n                <li><strong>70,008</strong> = Seventy thousand, eight</li>\n                <li><strong>99,999</strong> = Ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred ninety-nine</li>\n            </ul>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Reading and writing large numbers becomes effortless when we use periods and commas according to the Indian system of numeration. In our Indian system, the digits are grouped into families called periods. The first three digits from the right—Ones, Tens, and Hundreds—form the Ones Period. To the left of the Hundreds place, we place our very first comma! The next two digits—Thousands and Ten Thousands—form the Thousands Period. This comma acts as a natural speaking pause. When reading a number, we read all the digits in the Thousands period together, say the word thousand, pause at the comma, and then read the remaining three digits in the Ones period. For example, look at the number four, eight, comma, two, one, five. The digits four and eight are in the Thousands period, making forty-eight thousand. After the comma, two, one, five is read as two hundred fifteen. So the complete number name is forty-eight thousand two hundred fifteen! If there are zeros, such as seven, zero, comma, zero, zero, eight, we read it simply as seventy thousand eight. Try entering different five-digit numbers in the comma formatter on your screen and listen to how neatly they are spoken and written!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Where is the comma correctly placed in the Indian system for the number 58240?', 'options': ['5,8240', '58,240', '582,40', '5824,0'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'In the Indian system, the first comma is placed after 3 digits from the right (after the hundreds digit): 58,240.', 'study_insight': "58,240 is read as 'Fifty-eight thousand, two hundred forty'."}, {'question': 'What is the correct number name for the numeral 80,050?', 'options': ['Eight thousand fifty', 'Eighty thousand, five hundred', 'Eighty thousand, fifty', 'Eight hundred thousand fifty'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': "80 is in the thousands period (eighty thousand), 0 in hundreds, and 50 in tens and ones. So it is read as 'Eighty thousand, fifty'.", 'study_insight': 'Zeroes act as place holders. Even if a place has zero, it preserves the positional value of the surrounding digits.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Ordering Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Comparing 5-Digit Numbers and Sorting in Increasing and Decreasing Order', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When planning travel routes or checking train odometer readings, we often compare numbers to see which distance is greater or sort them from shortest to longest.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1.5">Rules for Comparing 5-Digit Numbers</span>\n                <ol class="text-xs text-slate-300 list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">\n                    <li><strong>Compare Highest Place First:</strong> Look at the Ten Thousands (TTh) digit. The number with the greater TTh digit is larger (e.g., <strong>6</strong>2,400 > <strong>4</strong>8,900).</li>\n                    <li><strong>If TTh digits are equal:</strong> Compare the Thousands (Th) digits (e.g., 5<strong>8</strong>,200 > 5<strong>3</strong>,400).</li>\n                    <li><strong>If Th digits are equal:</strong> Move to Hundreds, then Tens, and finally Ones.</li>\n                </ol>\n            </div>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-2 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-950/50 border border-sky-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-sky-300 block mb-1">Increasing (Ascending) Order</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">From smallest to largest:<br>12,450 < 23,800 < 45,120 < 78,900</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-300 block mb-1">Decreasing (Descending) Order</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">From largest to smallest:<br>78,900 > 45,120 > 23,800 > 12,450</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'When planning travels and comparing journey logs, we often need to sort multi-digit numbers from smallest to greatest, which is ascending order, or from greatest to smallest, which is descending order. To compare two or more five-digit numbers, we follow a simple, golden rule: always compare the digits starting from the highest place value column, which is the Ten Thousands place! If the digits in the Ten Thousands place are different, the number with the greater digit is immediately the larger number. For instance, seventy-eight thousand two hundred is greater than thirty-four thousand five hundred because seven is greater than three. But what if the Ten Thousands digits are identical? Then we move one step to the right and compare the Thousands digits. If those are also equal, we compare the Hundreds digits, followed by Tens, and finally Ones. Look at the vehicle odometer logs on your screen. We have a delivery van at twelve thousand nine hundred kilometres, a car at thirty-four thousand five hundred, a bus at seventy-eight thousand two hundred, and a highway truck at ninety-five thousand four hundred. Use the sorting buttons to toggle between ascending and descending order, and notice how comparing the leftmost digits guarantees a correct ordering every single time!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which comparison symbol correctly fits in the blank: 45,820 ___ 45,790?', 'options': ['< (less than)', '> (greater than)', '= (equal to)', '≈ (approximately equal)'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Both numbers have 4 in TTh and 5 in Th. In the Hundreds place, 8 is greater than 7. Therefore, 45,820 > 45,790.', 'study_insight': 'When the higher place value digits match, the next place value to the right breaks the tie.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following lists is sorted in strictly increasing (ascending) order?', 'options': ['34,500; 32,100; 45,000', '15,200; 18,400; 25,600; 42,100', '67,800; 54,300; 21,900; 10,000', '50,000; 40,000; 60,000'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '15,200 < 18,400 < 25,600 < 42,100 is arranged from smallest to largest, which is ascending order.', 'study_insight': 'Ascending order goes up like climbing stairs, while descending order goes down like descending a mountain.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Digit Swap', 'subtitle': 'Digit Exchange Puzzles and Making Largest and Smallest Numbers', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">What happens when you swap two digits in a number? Because of our positional place value system, swapping digits drastically alters the number\'s total value!</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Power of Position</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Take the number <strong>25,480</strong>. If you swap the digits in the Ten Thousands (2) and Thousands (5) places, you get <strong>52,480</strong>.<br>The value increased by: 52,480 − 25,480 = <strong>27,000</strong>!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-2 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-950/50 border border-sky-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-sky-300 block mb-1">Forming the Largest Number</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">Arrange given digits in <strong>descending order</strong> (highest digit in highest place):<br>Digits 3, 8, 1, 9, 5 → <strong>98,531</strong></p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-300 block mb-1">Forming the Smallest Number</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">Arrange digits in <strong>ascending order</strong> (if 0 is present, put it in second place):<br>Digits 4, 0, 7, 2, 9 → <strong>20,479</strong> (not 02,479!)</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Here is a captivating mathematical puzzle that reveals the true power of place values: what happens when you swap the positions of two digits in a large number? The face value of each digit remains the same, but because its place value changes, the entire number can increase or decrease dramatically! Let us investigate the odometer reading twenty-five thousand four hundred eighty. Here, the digit two is in the Ten Thousands place, worth twenty thousand, and the digit five is in the Thousands place, worth five thousand. Now, imagine swapping the two and the five! The new number becomes fifty-two thousand four hundred eighty. Notice what happened: the five is now in the Ten Thousands place, worth fifty thousand, while the two is in the Thousands place, worth two thousand. The new number is greater than the original by exactly twenty-seven thousand! Conversely, if you swap two digits where the smaller digit moves to a higher place, the total value drops. Test this on the interactive number shifter on your screen. Click the swap buttons, observe the place value transformations, and see how calculating the difference helps us solve real-world mathematical mystery puzzles!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Using the digits 7, 2, 9, 4, and 1 each exactly once, what is the largest 5-digit number you can form?', 'options': ['97,421', '97,241', '94,721', '12,479'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'To make the greatest number, arrange digits in descending order: 9 > 7 > 4 > 2 > 1 → 97,421.', 'study_insight': "Placing the largest available digit in the highest place value maximizes the number's total value."}, {'question': 'Using the digits 6, 0, 8, 3, and 5 each once, what is the smallest 5-digit number you can form?', 'options': ['03,568', '30,568', '35,068', '30,658'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'We cannot put 0 in the first place because that would create a 4-digit number. The next smallest non-zero digit is 3, followed by 0, then 5, 6, 8 → 30,568.', 'study_insight': 'A leading zero does not count as a digit in whole numbers, so 03,568 is only a 4-digit number (3,568).'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Nearest Tens', 'subtitle': 'Rounding Off Numbers to the Nearest 10 Using Number Lines', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In everyday travel, we often use approximate numbers. For example, if a bus has 48 passengers, we say it has "about 50 passengers". This practical mathematical skill is called <strong>Rounding Off</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Rabbit and Carrot Number Line</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Imagine a rabbit sitting on number <strong>43</strong> between two carrot patches at tens: <strong>40</strong> and <strong>50</strong>.<br>Distance to 40 = 3 hops. Distance to 50 = 7 hops.<br>The rabbit will hop to the closer food at <strong>40</strong>! So 43 rounded to nearest 10 is <strong>40</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-sky-200">\n                <strong>The Halfway Rule:</strong> Look at the <em>Ones digit</em>:<br>\n                • If the Ones digit is <strong>0, 1, 2, 3, or 4</strong> (less than 5) → Round DOWN to the previous ten.<br>\n                • If the Ones digit is <strong>5, 6, 7, 8, or 9</strong> (5 or more) → Round UP to the next ten.<br>\n                <em>Example:</em> 45 is exactly halfway between 40 and 50. By mathematical convention, we round <strong>UP to 50</strong>.\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'In everyday travel, we frequently use approximate numbers instead of exact measurements. For example, if a bus has forty-eight passengers, we might say it has about fifty passengers. This practical skill is called rounding off! To round a number to the nearest ten, we imagine a number line marked in multiples of ten, such as forty and fifty. Picture a hungry rabbit standing at a position between forty and fifty. At forty, there is a bundle of carrots, and at fifty, there is another bundle. The rabbit naturally wants to hop to the nearest food! The decision depends entirely on the digit in the Ones column. If the Ones digit is one, two, three, or four, the number is closer to the lower ten, so we round down. For example, forty-three is closer to forty than to fifty, so forty-three rounds down to forty. But if the Ones digit is five, six, seven, eight, or nine, the number is closer to the higher ten, so we round up. When a number sits exactly in the middle with a five, such as forty-five, mathematical convention always rounds up to fifty! Drag the rabbit on your screen to different positions and observe how the Ones digit determines the nearest ten!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 76 rounded to the nearest ten (10)?', 'options': ['70', '75', '80', '86'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The Ones digit of 76 is 6 (which is 5 or more), so we round UP to the next ten: 80.', 'study_insight': 'On a number line, 76 is only 4 steps away from 80, but 6 steps away from 70. 80 is the nearest ten.'}, {'question': 'A train coach has 64 seats booked. Rounded to the nearest ten, how many seats are booked?', 'options': ['60', '65', '70', '100'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The Ones digit is 4 (less than 5), so 64 is closer to 60 than to 70. Rounded to the nearest 10, it is 60.', 'study_insight': 'Numbers ending in 1, 2, 3, and 4 round down to the previous multiple of ten.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Nearest Hundreds', 'subtitle': 'Rounding to the Nearest 100 Using Benchmarks and Highway Milestones', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When travellers drive down a highway, milestones often show distances rounded to hundreds of kilometres. To round a number to the <strong>nearest hundred</strong>, we find which multiple of 100 it is closest to.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Highway Milestone Benchmarks</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Multiples of 100 are: 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 1,000, etc.<br>Between 300 and 400, the exact halfway mark is <strong>350</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>Rule for Nearest 100:</strong> Look at the <em>Tens digit</em>:<br>\n                • If the last two digits are <strong>00 to 49</strong> (Tens digit < 5) → Round DOWN to the lower hundred.<br>\n                • If the last two digits are <strong>50 to 99</strong> (Tens digit ≥ 5) → Round UP to the higher hundred.<br>\n                <em>Examples:</em><br>\n                • <strong>328</strong> → Tens digit is 2 (< 5) → rounds to <strong>300</strong>.<br>\n                • <strong>372</strong> → Tens digit is 7 (≥ 5) → rounds to <strong>400</strong>.<br>\n                • <strong>350</strong> → Exactly halfway → rounds UP to <strong>400</strong>.\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us take our rounding skills to the highway and learn how to round off to the nearest hundred! Imagine driving along a long national highway where major milestone markers are placed every one hundred kilometres, such as at three hundred kilometres and four hundred kilometres. When we round to the nearest hundred, which digit holds the secret key? It is the digit in the Tens place! We examine the number formed by the last two digits: Tens and Ones. If the Tens digit is zero, one, two, three, or four—meaning the remaining value is between zero and forty-nine—the number is closer to the lower hundred, so we round down. For example, three hundred twenty-eight kilometres has a two in the Tens place. Because twenty-eight is less than fifty, three hundred twenty-eight rounds down to three hundred kilometres. However, if the Tens digit is five or more—meaning the remaining value is fifty to ninety-nine—we round up to the next hundred. For instance, three hundred fifty and three hundred seventy-two both round up to four hundred kilometres! Test this on the interactive highway car simulator by selecting different distances and watching the milestone marker snap to the nearest hundred!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 436 rounded to the nearest hundred (100)?', 'options': ['400', '430', '440', '500'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The Tens digit is 3 (less than 5), and 436 is closer to 400 (36 away) than to 500 (64 away). It rounds down to 400.', 'study_insight': 'Check the tens digit: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 round down; 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 round up.'}, {'question': 'A village bus travelled 750 km on a state tour. Rounded to the nearest hundred, what is the distance?', 'options': ['700 km', '750 km', '800 km', '1,000 km'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '750 is exactly halfway between 700 and 800. By mathematical rule, halfway numbers round UP to the higher hundred: 800 km.', 'study_insight': 'The halfway rule ensures consistency across mathematics and everyday trade.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Nearest Thousands', 'subtitle': 'Rounding to 1,000 and When Rounding to 100 Gives the Same Result', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To round a number to the <strong>nearest thousand</strong>, we check which multiple of 1,000 it is closest to on a number line (e.g., 1,000, 2,000, 3,000, 4,000, etc.). The halfway mark between consecutive thousands is <strong>500</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Rule for Nearest 1,000</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Look at the <em>Hundreds digit</em>:<br>\n                • If Hundreds digit is <strong>0, 1, 2, 3, or 4</strong> (< 500) → Round DOWN to lower thousand.<br>\n                • If Hundreds digit is <strong>5, 6, 7, 8, or 9</strong> (≥ 500) → Round UP to higher thousand.<br>\n                <em>Example:</em> In <strong>2,346</strong>, hundreds digit is 3 (< 5) → rounds to <strong>2,000</strong>.<br>\n                In <strong>7,820</strong>, hundreds digit is 8 (≥ 5) → rounds to <strong>8,000</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-amber-200">\n                <strong>Let Us Think (Fascinating Puzzle):</strong> Can rounding a number to the nearest 100 give the <em>exact same answer</em> as rounding it to the nearest 1,000?<br>\n                <strong>YES!</strong> Take <strong>2,980</strong>:<br>\n                • Rounded to nearest 100 → <strong>3,000</strong> (since 80 ≥ 50, rounds up from 2,900 to 3,000).<br>\n                • Rounded to nearest 1,000 → <strong>3,000</strong> (since 980 ≥ 500, rounds up from 2,000 to 3,000).<br>\n                Both give 3,000!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'When measuring long train journeys or aeroplane flights across India, we round distances to the nearest thousand! To round to the nearest thousand, we inspect the digit in the Hundreds place. If the Hundreds digit is five, six, seven, eight, or nine—meaning five hundred or more—we round up to the next thousand. If it is less than five, we round down. For example, two thousand eight hundred fifty rounds up to three thousand. Now, here is a brilliant mathematical curiosity that will make you think like a true mathematician: can rounding a number to the nearest hundred give the exact same milestone as rounding it to the nearest thousand? Yes, it can! Consider the number two thousand nine hundred eighty. When rounding to the nearest hundred, we look at the Tens digit, which is eight. Since eight is greater than five, two thousand nine hundred eighty rounds up to three thousand! Now round the same number, two thousand nine hundred eighty, to the nearest thousand: we look at the Hundreds digit, which is nine. Since nine is greater than five, it rounds up to three thousand as well! Both roundings land on three thousand! Use the dual-rounding analyzer on your screen to test numbers like four thousand nine hundred sixty and seven thousand nine hundred seventy-five, and explore this wonderful mathematical harmony!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 4,625 rounded to the nearest thousand (1,000)?', 'options': ['4,000', '4,600', '5,000', '4,500'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The Hundreds digit of 4,625 is 6 (which is 5 or more), so 4,625 is closer to 5,000 than to 4,000. It rounds UP to 5,000.', 'study_insight': '625 is greater than the halfway mark of 500, pulling the number up to the next thousand.'}, {'question': 'Which of these numbers gives 4,000 when rounded to the nearest 100 AND also 4,000 when rounded to the nearest 1,000?', 'options': ['3,420', '3,965', '4,450', '4,850'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'For 3,965: Rounded to nearest 100 gives 4,000 (3,965 rounds up from 3,900 to 4,000). Rounded to nearest 1,000 also gives 4,000 (965 ≥ 500).', 'study_insight': 'Numbers near 4,000 with high hundreds digits (like 3,950 to 3,999) round to 4,000 in both hundred and thousand roundings.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Transport Speeds', 'subtitle': 'Comparing Speeds in km/h and Distance = Speed × Time Calculations', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Different vehicles travel at vastly different speeds. Speed tells us <strong>how many kilometres a traveller covers in one hour (km/h)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="overflow-x-auto my-3">\n                <table class="w-full text-center text-xs border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">\n                    <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-amber-400 font-bold">\n                        <tr>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Mode of Transport</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Average Speed</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Distance in 5 Hours</th>\n                        </tr>\n                    </thead>\n                    <tbody class="bg-slate-950/70 text-slate-200">\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Walking on Foot</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-sky-400">4 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">4 × 5 = <strong>20 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Bullock Cart / Camel</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-sky-400">5 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">5 × 5 = <strong>25 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Express Passenger Train</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-emerald-400">80 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">80 × 5 = <strong>400 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Commercial Aeroplane</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-amber-400">800 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">800 × 5 = <strong>4,000 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Spacecraft in Orbit</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-purple-400">28,000 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">28,000 × 5 = <strong>140,000 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                    </tbody>\n                </table>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300"><strong>Golden Travel Formula:</strong> <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold font-mono">Distance = Speed × Time</span>. If you know how fast you travel and for how many hours, you can calculate the total distance covered!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Speed tells us how fast an object moves, measured as the distance covered in one hour, written as kilometres per hour or km/h. To calculate the total distance covered during any journey, we use the fundamental mathematical formula: Distance equals Speed multiplied by Time! For example, if a passenger train travels at a speed of eighty kilometres per hour for four hours, the total distance covered is eighty multiplied by four, which equals three hundred twenty kilometres. If you travel on a modern superfast train at one hundred twenty kilometres per hour for five hours, you cover one hundred twenty multiplied by five, which equals six hundred kilometres! Now think about an aeroplane flying at eight hundred kilometres per hour. In just three hours, it covers eight hundred multiplied by three, which equals two thousand four hundred kilometres, flying almost the entire length of India from north to south! And what about an orbital spacecraft flying at twenty-eight thousand kilometres per hour? In two hours, it covers an astounding fifty-six thousand kilometres, circling more than the entire circumference of Earth! Experiment with the interactive flight deck on your screen: select different vehicles, adjust the travel duration slider from one to twelve hours, and watch the distance formula calculate every journey in real time!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A high-speed train travels at a constant speed of 120 km/h. How far will it travel in 3 hours?', 'options': ['240 km', '360 km', '400 km', '480 km'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Distance = Speed × Time = 120 km/h × 3 h = 360 km.', 'study_insight': 'Multiplying the distance covered in 1 hour by the number of hours gives total distance.'}, {'question': 'An aeroplane flies at 850 km/h. How many hours will it take to complete a non-stop flight of 3,400 km?', 'options': ['2 hours', '3 hours', '4 hours', '5 hours'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Time = Distance ÷ Speed = 3,400 ÷ 850 = 4 hours.', 'study_insight': 'Division is the inverse operation of multiplication: 850 × 4 = 3,400.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Trip Planning', 'subtitle': 'Vehicle Capacity Math: Calculating Buses and Vans for School Field Trips', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Planning a school excursion or railway tour requires practical division, multiplication, and remainder management. Let us solve a real-world field trip scenario:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Scenario: Grade 5 Nature Excursion</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A total of <strong>210 students and teachers</strong> are going on a study trip to a wildlife reserve.<br>\n                • A large bus can carry <strong>50 passengers</strong>.<br>\n                • A mini-van can carry <strong>15 passengers</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-sky-200 space-y-1">\n                <strong>Step 1:</strong> How many full 50-seater buses can we fill?<br>\n                210 ÷ 50 = <strong>4 full buses</strong> (carrying 4 × 50 = 200 passengers).<br>\n                <strong>Step 2:</strong> Remaining passengers = 210 − 200 = <strong>10 passengers</strong>.<br>\n                <strong>Step 3:</strong> Can 10 passengers fit in one 15-seater mini-van? <strong>Yes!</strong><br>\n                <strong>Optimal Fleet:</strong> 4 Buses and 1 Mini-van (with 5 empty spare seats).\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">If we hired only 50-seater buses, we would need <strong>5 buses</strong> because 4 buses only hold 200, leaving 10 behind!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Planning a school excursion requires sharp mathematical thinking to ensure that every student and teacher has a comfortable seat without wasting money on empty vehicles! Imagine our school is organizing a field trip for one hundred thirty-five travellers. Standard school buses have fifty seats each, while smaller mini-vans have fifteen seats each. How many vehicles should the school hire? Let us divide! First, we divide one hundred thirty-five by fifty. Fifty goes into one hundred thirty-five two times, because fifty multiplied by two equals one hundred. This leaves a remainder of thirty-five passengers. So, the school books two large fifty-seater buses, carrying one hundred passengers. Now, what do we do with the remaining thirty-five passengers? We divide thirty-five by fifteen. Fifteen multiplied by two is thirty, which leaves five students without seats! We cannot leave anyone behind, so we must round up and book three mini-vans, which provide forty-five seats. Two large buses plus three mini-vans provide a total of one hundred forty-five seats, seating all one hundred thirty-five passengers safely with ten extra seats to spare! Slide the headcount slider on your screen from twenty to two hundred fifty passengers to see how mathematics dispatches the perfect fleet for every journey!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A school has 145 students going on a museum visit. If each bus holds 40 students, how many buses are needed so that no student is left behind?', 'options': ['3 buses', '4 buses', '5 buses', '10 buses'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '145 ÷ 40 = 3 with a remainder of 25 students. 3 buses seat only 120 students. To seat all 145 students, a 4th bus is necessary.', 'study_insight': 'In real-world transport problems with remainders, we must round UP to ensure everyone has a seat.'}, {'question': 'If each ticket for a 45-seater bus costs ₹120, what is the total ticket collection for a completely full bus?', 'options': ['₹4,500', '₹5,400', '₹6,000', '₹4,800'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Total collection = 45 seats × ₹120 = ₹5,400.', 'study_insight': '45 × 120 = 45 × 12 × 10 = 540 × 10 = ₹5,400.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Everyday Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Finding 4-Digit and 5-Digit Numbers Around Us in Real Life', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Large numbers are not just written on classroom chalkboards; they exist all around us in daily life, nature, literature, and community planning.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Words in a Book</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A typical children\'s storybook has about 150 words per page. A 100-page novel contains approximately <strong>15,000 words</strong>!</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Trees in an Orchard / Forest</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A community forest reserve with 250 rows of 50 trees each holds 250 × 50 = <strong>12,500 shade and fruit trees</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-2">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">School Campus Students</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A large school cluster across 30 sections with 40 students each educates 30 × 40 = <strong>1,200 students</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-purple-400 block mb-1">Town Population Census</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A growing highway junction township has 8,500 households with an average of 4 members each = <strong>34,000 citizens</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Estimating and multiplying large numbers helps city planners build the right number of water tanks, hospitals, and parks!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Large five-digit numbers are not just abstract symbols in a math textbook; they are all around us in the real world every single day! Think about the book you are reading right now. A complete Joyful Mathematics textbook contains approximately eighteen thousand four hundred fifty words. That is a five-digit number where one is in Ten Thousands, eight is in Thousands, four is in Hundreds, five is in Tens, and zero is in Ones. Now picture a grand international cricket stadium during a match: there are forty-two thousand five hundred spectators cheering in the stands, which means four ten-thousands, two thousands, and five hundreds! When forest officers protect wildlife reserves, they count twenty-five thousand six hundred eighty trees in a protected sanctuary. And when governments conduct a census, a small bustling town may have sixty-eight thousand nine hundred residents! Understanding five-digit numbers allows engineers to build bridges, doctors to order medicines, and city planners to supply clean water and electricity to thousands of families. Tap on the different real-world scenarios on your screen to inspect their place value breakdowns and see how mathematics measures the vibrant world around us!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A dictionary has 400 pages with an average of 60 words on each page. Approximately how many total words are in the dictionary?', 'options': ['2,400 words', '24,000 words', '240,000 words', '240 words'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Total words = 400 pages × 60 words/page = 24,000 words.', 'study_insight': 'Mental math trick: 4 × 6 = 24, then attach the three zeroes (two from 400, one from 60) to get 24,000.'}, {'question': 'A stadium has 25 rows of seats, and each row has 80 seats. How many spectators can sit in the stadium?', 'options': ['200', '1,500', '2,000', '20,000'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '25 rows × 80 seats = 2,000 seats.', 'study_insight': '25 × 8 = 200; 200 × 10 = 2,000.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'River Crossing', 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The Classic Boatman, Lion, Sheep, and Grass Puzzle', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">During long train journeys, travellers have enjoyed solving recreational math brainteasers for centuries! One of the most famous river-crossing logic puzzles dates back over 1,200 years:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Dilemma</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A boatman must ferry a <strong>lion</strong>, a <strong>sheep</strong>, and a <strong>bundle of grass</strong> across a river in a small rowboat.<br>\n                • The boat can hold only the <strong>boatman and ONE item</strong> at a time.<br>\n                • <em>Danger 1:</em> If left alone without the boatman, the lion will eat the sheep!<br>\n                • <em>Danger 2:</em> If left alone without the boatman, the sheep will eat the grass bundle!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>The 7-Step Winning Solution:</strong><br>\n                1. Take the <strong>Sheep</strong> across (leaves Lion and Grass safely together).<br>\n                2. Row back empty.<br>\n                3. Take the <strong>Grass</strong> across.<br>\n                4. Bring the <strong>Sheep BACK</strong> to the start (crucial move! Don\'t leave Sheep with Grass).<br>\n                5. Take the <strong>Lion</strong> across (leaves Sheep alone at start; Lion & Grass are safe together).<br>\n                6. Row back empty.<br>\n                7. Take the <strong>Sheep</strong> across! All three arrive safely across the river!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'During long journeys across our country, travellers historically shared riddles, brainteasers, and puzzles to pass the time and sharpen their wits. One of the most famous puzzles in mathematical folklore is the River Crossing Puzzle! A boatman arrives at the riverbank with a lion, a sheep, and a bundle of lush green grass. He must ferry all three across to the opposite bank. However, his small boat can carry only himself and one item at a time. Furthermore, he faces two strict danger rules: if left alone together on either bank without the boatman, the lion will eat the sheep, and the sheep will eat the grass! How can he transport all three safely? The secret lies in a clever seven-step sequence! First, the boatman takes the sheep across, because the lion does not eat grass. Leaving the sheep on the far bank, he returns alone. Next, he takes the lion across. But to prevent the lion from eating the sheep, he brings the sheep BACK to the starting bank! Then, he leaves the sheep and takes the grass across to join the lion. Finally, he returns empty-handed to fetch the sheep one last time! Play this puzzle on your screen, test your logical deductions, and experience the joy of mathematical problem solving!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In the river crossing puzzle, which item MUST the boatman take across the river on his very first trip?', 'options': ['The Lion', 'The Grass bundle', 'The Sheep', 'He should row across empty'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'He must take the Sheep first. If he takes the Lion, the Sheep eats the Grass. If he takes the Grass, the Lion eats the Sheep. Leaving Lion and Grass together is completely safe!', 'study_insight': 'Identifying compatible pairs (Lion and Grass) is the key insight to unlocking constraint logic puzzles.'}, {'question': "What is the critical 'reverse move' on trip 4 that prevents the sheep from eating the grass on the far bank?", 'options': ['He leaves the boat behind and swims', 'He brings the Sheep back with him to the starting bank', 'He ties the lion to a tree', 'He feeds half the grass to the lion'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'On trip 4, after delivering the grass, the boatman rows the Sheep back to the starting bank. This prevents the sheep from eating the grass on the far side while allowing him to fetch the lion.', 'study_insight': 'In problem solving, moving backward temporarily is sometimes the only path to moving forward successfully.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Pebble Game', 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The 2-Player Nim Strategy and Multiples of 4', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When travellers sit together on long journeys, they often play strategy games using simple pebbles, coins, or matchsticks. One classic game is the <strong>Pile of Pebbles Game</strong>:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Game Rules</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">• Start with a pile of pebbles (e.g., <strong>15 pebbles</strong>).<br>\n                • Two players take turns taking <strong>1, 2, or 3 pebbles</strong> from the pile.<br>\n                • The player forced to take the <em>very last pebble loses</em> (or the player who takes the last pebble wins, depending on variant).</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>The Secret Mathematical Strategy: Multiples of 4!</strong><br>\n                Notice that 1 + 3 = 4, and 2 + 2 = 4. Whatever number <em>x</em> the opponent takes, you can always take <strong>4 − x</strong> so that every round removes exactly <strong>4 pebbles</strong>!<br>\n                • If the goal is to take the last pebble: Always leave a multiple of 4 for your opponent (4, 8, 12, 16...).<br>\n                • With 15 pebbles: Player 1 takes <strong>3 pebbles</strong>, leaving 12 (a multiple of 4). Whatever Player 2 takes, Player 1 complements to 4, guaranteeing victory!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Here is another timeless pastime played by travellers under the shade of banyan trees: the Strategic Pile of Pebbles Game! Two players sit beside a pile of fifteen pebbles. On each turn, a player may take one, two, or three pebbles from the pile. The player who picks up the very last pebble wins the game! Can you guarantee a win every single time? Yes, if you understand the mathematics of multiples of four! Notice that whatever number of pebbles your opponent takes—whether one, two, or three—you can always take enough pebbles so that the sum of both turns equals four! For example, if they take one, you take three. If they take two, you take two. If they take three, you take one. In every round, the pile decreases by exactly four pebbles! Since fifteen divided by four equals three with a remainder of three, the winning strategy is clear: on your very first turn, take exactly three pebbles! This leaves twelve pebbles, which is a multiple of four. From that moment on, whenever your opponent takes N pebbles, you take four minus N pebbles, ensuring you leave eight, then four, and finally take the last pebbles to victory! Challenge the computer on your screen and master the multiple-of-four strategy!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In the pebble game, if your opponent takes 2 pebbles on their turn, how many pebbles should you take to make the round total 4?', 'options': ['1 pebble', '2 pebbles', '3 pebbles', '4 pebbles'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'To make the round total 4, you take 4 − 2 = 2 pebbles.', 'study_insight': 'Complementary pairs: (1, 3), (2, 2), and (3, 1) all sum to 4.'}, {'question': 'If you start with 15 pebbles and want to leave a multiple of 4 on the table for your opponent, how many pebbles should you take on your very first turn?', 'options': ['1 pebble (leaving 14)', '2 pebbles (leaving 13)', '3 pebbles (leaving 12, which is 3 × 4)', 'None'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '15 − 3 = 12. 12 is a multiple of 4 (4 × 3 = 12). Leaving a multiple of 4 allows you to control the game to victory.', 'study_insight': '15 = (3 × 4) + 3. Removing the remainder of 3 secures the winning state.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Digit Difference', 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The Magic 9 Subtraction Mystery and Reversal Logic', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Pick any two different digits, say <strong>7</strong> and <strong>2</strong>. What happens when you explore their difference? Try this fascinating numerical trick:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3 space-y-1 text-xs">\n                <p class="text-slate-300">1. Form the two 2-digit numbers: <strong>72</strong> and <strong>27</strong>.</p>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">2. Subtract smaller from larger: 72 − 27 = <strong>45</strong>.</p>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">3. Now take the digits of 45 (4 and 5), form 54 and 45: 54 − 45 = <strong>9</strong>!</p>\n                <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold">Try another pair: Digits 8 and 3 → 83 − 38 = 45 → 54 − 45 = 9!</p>\n                <p class="text-sky-400 font-bold">Digits 9 and 1 → 91 − 19 = 72 → 72 − 27 = 45 → 54 − 45 = 9!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>Why does this always yield a multiple of 9?</strong><br>\n                Any two-digit number with digits <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> has value: 10<em>a</em> + <em>b</em>.<br>\n                Reversing the digits gives: 10<em>b</em> + <em>a</em>.<br>\n                Subtracting: (10<em>a</em> + <em>b</em>) − (10<em>b</em> + <em>a</em>) = 9<em>a</em> − 9<em>b</em> = <strong>9 × (a − b)</strong>!<br>\n                The difference is <em>always a multiple of 9</em>, which inexorably collapses down to 9!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Let us explore a delightful piece of number magic discovered by Indian mathematicians, including the legendary D. R. Kaprekar! Think of any two different digits, say eight and three. Use them to form a two-digit number: eighty-three. Now, reverse the digits to form thirty-eight. Next, subtract the smaller number from the larger number: eighty-three minus thirty-eight equals forty-five. Now examine forty-five: forty-five is a multiple of nine, because nine multiplied by five is forty-five! What is truly extraordinary is that the difference between the original digits, eight minus three, is five, and the answer is nine multiplied by five! Try it with any two digits you choose, like nine and two. Ninety-two minus twenty-nine is sixty-three, which is nine multiplied by seven, and nine minus two is seven! Why does this always work? Algebra gives us the beautiful proof: any two-digit number with tens digit a and ones digit b has the value ten a plus b. Its reverse is ten b plus a. When you subtract them, ten a minus a is nine a, and b minus ten b is minus nine b. This simplifies to nine times the quantity a minus b! It must always be a multiple of nine! Test different digits on the interactive engine and watch the magic of nine unfold!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Pick digits 6 and 2. What is 62 minus 26?', 'options': ['26', '36', '46', '56'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '62 − 26 = 36. Notice that 36 is 9 × 4, which is 9 × (6 − 2)!', 'study_insight': 'The difference between any 2-digit number and its reverse is always 9 times the difference between its digits: 9 × (6 − 2) = 36.'}, {'question': 'If you reverse the digits of 36 to get 63, what is 63 − 36?', 'options': ['18', '27', '9', '36'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '63 − 36 = 27. Reversing 27 gives 72, and 72 − 27 = 45, and 54 − 45 = 9!', 'study_insight': 'Repeated subtraction of reversed numbers always descends along multiples of 9 until it reaches 9.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': "King's Horses", 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The Stable Trick Puzzle and Corner-Sharing Logic', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">A classic recreational math story tells of a king who had a square stable with 9 rooms (a 3×3 grid). The king kept horses in the outer 8 rooms, leaving the center room empty. He checked that <strong>each of the 4 outer sides contained exactly 5 horses</strong>, believing 4 × 5 = 20 horses were safely in the stable.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Original Stable (20 Horses)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 font-mono">\n                        0 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; 0 &nbsp;→ Top = 5<br>\n                        5 &nbsp; [ ] 5 &nbsp;→ Middle sides = 5 each<br>\n                        0 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; 0 &nbsp;→ Bottom = 5<br>\n                        Total horses = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = <strong>20 horses</strong>.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Caretaker Steals 2 Horses (18 Horses)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 font-mono">\n                        1 &nbsp; 3 &nbsp; 1 &nbsp;→ Top = 1+3+1 = 5<br>\n                        4 &nbsp; [ ] 4 &nbsp;→ Left/Right = 1+4+0 = 5<br>\n                        0 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; 0 &nbsp;→ Bottom = 0+5+0 = 5<br>\n                        Total horses = 2 corners + 16 middles = <strong>18 horses</strong>!\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-sky-200">\n                <strong>The Secret Corner-Sharing Formula:</strong><br>\n                Let <em>C</em> be the total horses placed in the 4 corners.<br>\n                Since each corner horse is shared by two sides, it is counted twice:<br>\n                <span class="font-mono text-amber-300 font-bold">Total Horses in Stable = 20 − C</span>.<br>\n                • When C = 0 (no corner horses), Total = 20.<br>\n                • When C = 1 (1 horse in a corner), Total = 19 (1 horse stolen!).<br>\n                • When C = 2 (2 horses in corners), Total = 18 (2 horses stolen!).<br>\n                On every count, each side still equals 5!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "Our final journey puzzle is the legendary King's Horses Stable Trick! A king kept twenty fine horses in a square stable arranged in a three-by-three grid with an open courtyard in the middle. The king was very particular: every evening, he inspected the stable by counting the horses along each of the four outer sides, making sure that each side had exactly five horses! One day, a clever caretaker secretly sold two horses, leaving only eighteen horses. Yet, when the king counted all four sides that evening, he still counted five horses on every single side, without suspecting a thing! How did the caretaker pull off this trick? By using the mathematics of corner sharing! When a horse is placed in a corner room, it touches two different sides of the stable, so it is counted twice! If there are five horses in each side, the king calculates four times five, which is twenty. But the actual total number of horses is twenty minus C, where C is the number of horses placed in the four corners! By placing one horse in each of two corners, and adjusting the middle rooms, the caretaker made eighteen horses sum to five on all four sides! Explore the three stable configurations on your screen, verify each side sum, and discover the mathematical secret of corner sharing!", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In a square 3×3 stable, why does placing horses in the corner rooms allow fewer total horses to produce the same side totals?', 'options': ['Corner rooms have mirrors that duplicate horses', 'Each corner room is counted as part of two adjacent sides simultaneously', 'Corner horses eat less hay', 'Horses in middle rooms run away at night'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'A corner room touches two sides. Placing horses in corners allows them to be counted twice in the side sums, reducing the total horses needed to satisfy the side total.', 'study_insight': 'In geometry and graph theory, vertices shared by two edges contribute to both boundary constraints.'}, {'question': 'If the 4 sides of a square stable each sum to 5 horses, and the 4 corners contain a total of 2 horses, what is the actual total number of horses in the stable?', 'options': ['20 horses', '19 horses', '18 horses (20 − 2 = 18)', '16 horses'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Total horses = (4 sides × 5) − (horses in corners counted twice) = 20 − 2 = 18 horses.', 'study_insight': 'Formula: Total Horses = 20 − C, where C is the number of horses in the corners.'}]}]

QUIZ_QUESTIONS = [{'id': 1, 'question': 'What is the smallest 5-digit number?', 'options': ['1,000', '9,999', '10,000 (Ten Thousand)', '100,000'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '10,000 is the smallest 5-digit number. It is formed by adding 1 to 9,999 (the largest 4-digit number).', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Analysis:</strong><br>1. The largest 4-digit number is 9,999.<br>2. 9,999 + 1 = 10,000.<br>3. 10,000 has 5 digits (1, 0, 0, 0, 0), making it the smallest 5-digit number.'}, {'id': 2, 'question': 'In the number 84,392, what is the place value of the digit 8?', 'options': ['80', '800', '8,000', '80,000 (Eighty Thousand)'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'The digit 8 is in the Ten Thousands (TTh) place, so its place value is 8 × 10,000 = 80,000.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Analysis:</strong><br>Place value columns: TTh (8) | Th (4) | H (3) | T (9) | O (2).<br>8 × 10,000 = 80,000.'}, {'id': 3, 'question': 'Which of the following correctly shows the Indian comma placement for 74025?', 'options': ['7,4025', '74,025', '740,25', '7402,5'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'In the Indian system, the first comma is placed after 3 digits from the right, giving 74,025.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Comma Rule:</strong><br>Count 3 digits from right (5, 2, 0) and place the first comma: 74,025. It separates the Thousands period (74) from the Ones period (025).'}, {'id': 4, 'question': 'What is the expanded form of 52,408?', 'options': ['50,000 + 2,000 + 400 + 8', '5,000 + 200 + 40 + 8', '50,000 + 200 + 40 + 8', '50,000 + 2,000 + 40 + 8'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': '5 Ten Thousands (50,000) + 2 Thousands (2,000) + 4 Hundreds (400) + 0 Tens (0) + 8 Ones (8) = 50,000 + 2,000 + 400 + 8.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Expansion:</strong><br>5 × 10,000 = 50,000<br>2 × 1,000 = 2,000<br>4 × 100 = 400<br>0 × 10 = 0<br>8 × 1 = 8<br>Sum = 50,000 + 2,000 + 400 + 8.'}, {'id': 5, 'question': "Which number is 'Sixty thousand, four hundred five' written in standard numerals?", 'options': ['6,405', '60,450', '60,405', '604,005'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Thousands period = 60. Ones period = 405 (4 hundreds, 0 tens, 5 ones). So the numeral is 60,405.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Breakdown:</strong><br>Sixty thousand = 60,000<br>Four hundred five = 405<br>60,000 + 405 = 60,405.'}, {'id': 6, 'question': 'Which of the following is the greatest 5-digit number?', 'options': ['90,000', '99,990', '99,999', '100,000'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '99,999 is the largest 5-digit number. Adding 1 to it yields 100,000, which has 6 digits (One Lakh).', 'solution_html': '<strong>Analysis:</strong><br>Highest digit in every place: 9 in TTh, 9 in Th, 9 in H, 9 in T, 9 in O = 99,999.'}, {'id': 7, 'question': 'Using the digits 5, 1, 8, 3, and 0 each once, what is the smallest 5-digit number you can make?', 'options': ['01,358', '10,358', '13,058', '10,538'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '0 cannot be the leading digit. The smallest non-zero digit is 1, followed by 0, then 3, 5, 8. So the smallest number is 10,358.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Rule:</strong><br>1. Lowest non-zero digit for highest place (TTh) = 1.<br>2. Place 0 in second position (Th) = 0.<br>3. Arrange remaining digits in ascending order: 3, 5, 8.<br>4. Result = 10,358.'}, {'id': 8, 'question': 'If you swap the digits in the Thousands and Tens places of 43,875, what is the new number?', 'options': ['47,835', '34,875', '43,785', '48,375'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'In 43,875, Thousands digit is 3 and Tens digit is 7. Swapping 3 and 7 gives 47,835.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Swap Trace:</strong><br>Original: 4 [3] 8 [7] 5<br>Swap 3 and 7:<br>New: 4 [7] 8 [3] 5 = 47,835.'}, {'id': 9, 'question': 'Which of these numbers is strictly greater than 67,420?', 'options': ['67,399', '66,999', '67,419', '67,425'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': '67,425 has 5 in the Ones place compared to 0 in 67,420, making 67,425 > 67,420.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Digit Comparison:</strong><br>Both have 67,42x. Comparing Ones digits: 5 > 0, so 67,425 > 67,420.'}, {'id': 10, 'question': 'What is the value of 10 bundles of Ten Thousand (10 × 10,000)?', 'options': ['10,000', '100,000 (One Lakh)', '1,000,000', '50,000'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '10 × 10,000 = 100,000, which is One Lakh in the Indian place value system.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Base-10 Progression:</strong><br>10 × 10 = 100<br>10 × 100 = 1,000<br>10 × 1,000 = 10,000<br>10 × 10,000 = 100,000 (One Lakh).'}, {'id': 11, 'question': 'What is 83 rounded to the nearest ten (10)?', 'options': ['80', '85', '90', '100'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The Ones digit is 3 (less than 5), so 83 is closer to 80 than to 90. It rounds down to 80.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Number Line Hop:</strong><br>83 is 3 steps away from 80 and 7 steps away from 90. 80 is the nearest ten.'}, {'id': 12, 'question': 'What is 265 rounded to the nearest ten (10)?', 'options': ['260', '265', '270', '300'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The Ones digit is 5. By the halfway rule, 5 always rounds UP to the next multiple of ten: 270.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Halfway Rule:</strong><br>265 is midway between 260 and 270. Halfway numbers round UP to 270.'}, {'id': 13, 'question': 'What is 649 rounded to the nearest hundred (100)?', 'options': ['600', '640', '650', '700'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Look at the Tens digit: it is 4 (which is less than 5). Since 49 is less than the halfway mark of 50, 649 rounds down to 600.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Nearest 100 Check:</strong><br>Between 600 and 700, the midpoint is 650.<br>649 < 650, so it rounds down to 600.'}, {'id': 14, 'question': 'What is 1,850 rounded to the nearest hundred (100)?', 'options': ['1,800', '1,850', '1,900', '2,000'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The Tens digit is 5 (halfway mark 50). By convention, halfway numbers round UP to the higher hundred: 1,900.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>1,850 is midway between 1,800 and 1,900. Rounding up gives 1,900.'}, {'id': 15, 'question': 'What is 7,346 rounded to the nearest thousand (1,000)?', 'options': ['7,000', '7,300', '7,400', '8,000'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The Hundreds digit is 3 (less than 5). 346 is less than 500, so 7,346 rounds down to 7,000.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Thousands Rounding:</strong><br>Midpoint between 7,000 and 8,000 is 7,500.<br>7,346 < 7,500 → rounds down to 7,000.'}, {'id': 16, 'question': 'What is 5,780 rounded to the nearest thousand (1,000)?', 'options': ['5,000', '5,700', '5,800', '6,000'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'The Hundreds digit is 7 (5 or more). 780 is greater than 500, so 5,780 rounds up to 6,000.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Thousands Rounding:</strong><br>780 ≥ 500 → rounds UP to 6,000.'}, {'id': 17, 'question': 'Which of the following numbers rounds to 5,000 when rounded to the nearest 100 AND also to 5,000 when rounded to the nearest 1,000?', 'options': ['4,960', '4,200', '5,400', '5,800'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'For 4,960: Rounded to nearest 100 gives 5,000 (rounds up from 4,900). Rounded to nearest 1,000 also gives 5,000 (960 ≥ 500).', 'solution_html': '<strong>Dual-Rounding Check:</strong><br>1. Nearest 100 of 4,960: Tens digit is 6 ≥ 5 → rounds up to 5,000.<br>2. Nearest 1,000 of 4,960: Hundreds digit is 9 ≥ 5 → rounds up to 5,000.<br>Both match!'}, {'id': 18, 'question': 'What is the smallest whole number that rounds up to 300 when rounded to the nearest hundred?', 'options': ['249', '250', '299', '301'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '250 is the very first number whose tens digit is 5, which rounds UP to 300.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Range for 300:</strong><br>Numbers that round to 300 are from 250 to 349.<br>Smallest = 250; Largest = 349.'}, {'id': 19, 'question': 'What is the largest whole number that rounds down to 600 when rounded to the nearest hundred?', 'options': ['649', '650', '699', '640'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': '649 has a tens digit of 4, so it rounds down to 600. 650 would round up to 700.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Boundary Analysis:</strong><br>649 has tens digit 4 (< 5) → rounds to 600.<br>650 has tens digit 5 (≥ 5) → rounds to 700.<br>So 649 is the maximum.'}, {'id': 20, 'question': 'A village road odometer reading is 14,892 km. What is this distance rounded to the nearest thousand kilometres?', 'options': ['14,000 km', '14,800 km', '15,000 km', '20,000 km'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'In 14,892, the Hundreds digit is 8 (≥ 5). Rounding to the nearest thousand rounds 14,892 up to 15,000 km.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Rounding Check:</strong><br>Hundreds digit is 8 ≥ 5 → 14 Thousands rounds UP to 15 Thousands = 15,000 km.'}, {'id': 21, 'question': 'A car travels along a highway at an average speed of 65 km/h. How far will it travel in 4 hours?', 'options': ['240 km', '250 km', '260 km', '270 km'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Distance = Speed × Time = 65 km/h × 4 h = 260 km.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Calculation:</strong><br>65 × 4 = (60 × 4) + (5 × 4) = 240 + 20 = 260 km.'}, {'id': 22, 'question': 'A train covers a journey of 450 km in 5 hours at uniform speed. What is the speed of the train?', 'options': ['80 km/h', '90 km/h', '95 km/h', '100 km/h'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Speed = Distance ÷ Time = 450 km ÷ 5 h = 90 km/h.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>450 ÷ 5 = 90 km/h.'}, {'id': 23, 'question': 'How long will it take a person walking at 4 km/h to cover a total distance of 28 km?', 'options': ['5 hours', '6 hours', '7 hours', '8 hours'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Time = Distance ÷ Speed = 28 km ÷ 4 km/h = 7 hours.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>28 ÷ 4 = 7 hours.'}, {'id': 24, 'question': 'A school has 185 students going on an educational tour. Each bus has 50 seats. How many buses are needed so every student gets a seat?', 'options': ['3 buses', '4 buses', '5 buses', '6 buses'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '185 ÷ 50 = 3 with a remainder of 35 students. 3 buses seat only 150 students, leaving 35 without seats. So 4 buses are required.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Fleet Math:</strong><br>3 buses × 50 = 150 seats (35 students left behind).<br>4 buses × 50 = 200 seats (enough for all 185 students with 15 spare seats).'}, {'id': 25, 'question': 'If hiring one bus costs ₹3,500, what is the total cost of hiring 4 buses for the school excursion?', 'options': ['₹12,000', '₹13,500', '₹14,000', '₹15,000'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Total cost = 4 buses × ₹3,500 = ₹14,000.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>4 × 3,500 = 4 × 35 × 100 = 140 × 100 = ₹14,000.'}, {'id': 26, 'question': 'An orchard has 40 rows of orange trees, with 35 trees in each row. How many total orange trees are in the orchard?', 'options': ['140 trees', '1,200 trees', '1,400 trees', '14,000 trees'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Total trees = 40 rows × 35 trees/row = 1,400 trees.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>40 × 35 = 4 × 35 × 10 = 140 × 10 = 1,400 trees.'}, {'id': 27, 'question': 'A library has 80 shelves, each holding 125 storybooks. How many total books are on the shelves?', 'options': ['1,000 books', '10,000 books', '12,500 books', '100,000 books'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Total books = 80 × 125 = 10,000 books (Ten Thousand books!).', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>8 × 125 = 1,000.<br>80 × 125 = 10,000 books.'}, {'id': 28, 'question': 'A bullet train travels at 320 km/h. How far does it travel in 2 hours and 30 minutes (2.5 hours)?', 'options': ['640 km', '720 km', '800 km', '960 km'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Distance = 320 km/h × 2.5 h = (320 × 2) + (320 × 0.5) = 640 + 160 = 800 km.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>320 × 2 = 640 km<br>320 × 0.5 = 160 km<br>640 + 160 = 800 km.'}, {'id': 29, 'question': 'A spacecraft travels at 28,000 km/h. How far does it travel in 3 hours of orbital flight?', 'options': ['56,000 km', '84,000 km', '100,000 km', '840,000 km'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Distance = 28,000 × 3 = 84,000 km.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Calculation:</strong><br>28 × 3 = 84.<br>28,000 × 3 = 84,000 km.'}, {'id': 30, 'question': 'A small town has a population of 24,650. Over one year, 1,350 new residents move in. What is the new population?', 'options': ['25,000', '26,000', '26,500', '25,900'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '24,650 + 1,350 = 26,000.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Sum:</strong><br>24,650 + 1,350 = 24,000 + 1,000 + 650 + 350 = 25,000 + 1,000 = 26,000.'}, {'id': 31, 'question': 'In the river crossing puzzle, why can the boatman NOT leave the lion and the sheep alone together?', 'options': ['The sheep will tease the lion', 'The lion will eat the sheep', 'The lion will jump into the river', 'The boat will sink'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Being a carnivore, the lion will prey upon the sheep if the boatman is not there to protect it.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Predator-Prey Constraint:</strong><br>Lion + Sheep without human presence = Lion eats Sheep.'}, {'id': 32, 'question': 'In the river crossing puzzle, why is it safe to leave the lion and the bundle of grass alone on the riverbank?', 'options': ['Lions are strictly carnivorous and do not eat grass', 'The grass is too heavy for the lion', 'The lion is afraid of green grass', 'The boatman ties the grass to the lion'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Lions do not eat grass! Therefore, leaving the lion and the grass bundle together on either bank is completely safe.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Compatibility Insight:</strong><br>Lion + Grass = Safe combination! This allows the boatman to transport the sheep first.'}, {'id': 33, 'question': 'In the 2-player pebble game (take 1, 2, or 3 pebbles per turn), what is the key number that forms the winning strategy?', 'options': ['Multiples of 2', 'Multiples of 3', 'Multiples of 4', 'Multiples of 5'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Multiples of 4! Because 1 + 3 = 4 and 2 + 2 = 4, a player can always complement whatever the opponent takes to reach a total of 4.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Complement Strategy:</strong><br>Opponent takes 1 → You take 3 (Total = 4)<br>Opponent takes 2 → You take 2 (Total = 4)<br>Opponent takes 3 → You take 1 (Total = 4)<br>Every full round eliminates exactly 4 pebbles.'}, {'id': 34, 'question': 'If you are playing the pebble game with 16 pebbles where taking the last pebble wins, should you go first or second to guarantee a win?', 'options': ['Go first and take 2 pebbles', 'Go second, because 16 is already a multiple of 4, so Player 2 can mirror Player 1 to maintain multiples of 4', 'It does not matter, the game is purely luck', 'Go first and take 4 pebbles'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "16 is already a multiple of 4 (4 × 4 = 16). Player 2 can always complement Player 1's moves to 4, guaranteeing Player 2 takes the last pebble.", 'solution_html': '<strong>Mathematical Proof:</strong><br>Since 16 is a multiple of 4, the second player has the winning strategy by maintaining the multiple-of-4 invariant.'}, {'id': 35, 'question': 'Pick digits 9 and 4. Form 94 and 49. What is 94 − 49?', 'options': ['35', '45', '54', '49'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '94 − 49 = 45. Notice that 45 is 9 × 5, where 5 is the difference between digits 9 and 4!', 'solution_html': '<strong>Proof:</strong><br>94 − 49 = 45 = 9 × (9 − 4) = 9 × 5 = 45.'}, {'id': 36, 'question': 'In the digit-difference puzzle, why does the difference between any 2-digit number and its reversal always equal a multiple of 9?', 'options': ['Because 9 is the largest single-digit number', 'Because (10a + b) − (10b + a) = 9a − 9b = 9 × (a − b)', 'Because all odd numbers are multiples of 9', 'It only works on Tuesdays'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Algebraic proof: (10a + b) − (10b + a) = 9a − 9b = 9(a − b). In base-10, shifting place values by 1 always produces multiples of 9.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Algebraic Proof:</strong><br>Let number = 10a + b.<br>Reversed number = 10b + a.<br>Difference = (10a + b) − (10b + a) = 9a − 9b = 9(a − b).'}, {'id': 37, 'question': "In the King's Horses puzzle, how many rooms are along each side of the square 3×3 stable?", 'options': ['2 rooms', '3 rooms (Corner, Middle, Corner)', '4 rooms', '5 rooms'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'A 3×3 grid has 3 rooms along each of its 4 sides: two corner rooms and one middle room.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Stable Geometry:</strong><br>Top side: Top-Left Corner + Top Middle + Top-Right Corner = 3 rooms.'}, {'id': 38, 'question': 'Why did moving horses from the middle rooms into the corner rooms allow the caretaker to steal horses while keeping 5 horses on each side?', 'options': ['Corner horses eat less hay', 'Each corner room is counted as part of two sides at the same time', 'The king forgot how to count to five', 'The horses changed colour'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Corner rooms are shared by two adjacent sides. Each horse placed in a corner contributes to the count of both sides simultaneously.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Corner-Sharing Logic:</strong><br>A horse in a corner room is counted twice—once when counting the horizontal side and once when counting the vertical side!'}, {'id': 39, 'question': 'In the original stable arrangement, each of the 4 middle rooms had 5 horses and corners had 0 horses. What was the total count of horses?', 'options': ['10 horses', '15 horses', '20 horses (4 × 5 = 20)', '25 horses'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '4 middle rooms × 5 horses each = 20 horses. Each side had 0 + 5 + 0 = 5 horses.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Original Verification:</strong><br>Side sum = 0 + 5 + 0 = 5.<br>Total horses = 4 middles × 5 = 20 horses.'}, {'id': 40, 'question': 'If each side of a 3×3 square stable must sum to 5 horses, and a caretaker places a total of 4 horses in the corners (1 in each of the 4 corners), what is the total number of horses in the stable?', 'options': ['12 horses', '14 horses', '16 horses (20 − 4 = 16)', '20 horses'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Each side has 1 in corner + 3 in middle + 1 in corner = 5 horses. Total horses = 4 corners × 1 + 4 middles × 3 = 4 + 12 = 16 horses (which equals 20 − 4 = 16)!', 'solution_html': '<strong>Formula Verification:</strong><br>Total Horses = 20 − C (where C is corner horses).<br>With C = 4 (1 in each corner):<br>Each middle needs 5 − (1 + 1) = 3 horses.<br>Total = 4(1) + 4(3) = 4 + 12 = 16 horses!'}]
